  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 228 AM CDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nMuch cooler and drier air will begin spilling into the region\nthrough Thursday night. Seasonably cool, below normal\ntemperatures extend through the upcoming weekend before\nmoderating back above normal early next week. \n\nA long wave trough advances east-southeast from the southern\nCanadian Provinces and over the Great Lakes Thursday night into\nFriday morning. RAP guidance shows strong cold air advections\nwith a 15-20C drop in mid-level temperatures as the axis over\nthe trough crosses the region between 06z-12z. Behind the\ntrough a strong surface high sets the the stage for\nnorthwesterly flow and rapidly cooling temperatures through\nFriday morning. Friday morning temperatures may even be somewhat\ndeceiving for early-risers as southeast sections of the CWA\nremain in the 50s/low-60s, only to continue to cool as we \nprogress through Friday morning. Temperatures aren't likely to\nrecover much from there with partial cloud cover and cold air \nadvection. While both Friday and Saturday temperatures are \nfairly similar (mid-50s/low-60s). The primary focus will be on \nSaturday and Sunday morning lows as NBM guidance is likely too \ncold with mid-30s being too broadly represented. This is due in \npart to prior guidance showing the surface high overhead \nSaturday morning, which now is further west, centered over the \neastern Plains. The surface high moves east into the Ohio Valley\nand mid-Atlantic Region by Sunday morning. Suffice to say, some\nof the coolest fall temperatures will still be felt and the \nnarrowing in the IQR data for each morning lends better \nconfidence for low-40s being well represented with mid-30s \nconfined to micro climate behavior that is typically more \nisolated. I would not be surprised to see NBM guidance inch \nupward with morning low over the next couple of days.\n\nThe moderating trend continues into early next week, though \nthere is enough spread (5-7 degrees) in the long range guidance\nto lower confidence in the magnitude of warmth. That said, most\nareas do return to the 70s Monday into Tuesday.",
